WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.401
When you deploy your agent, you need

2
00:00:01.401 --> 00:00:03.003
to make sure it has the right permissions

3
00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:05.071
to access only the data it needs.

4
00:00:05.372 --> 00:00:07.874
In this lesson, you'll learn what Databricks identity

5
00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:09.743
you need to assign to your agent

6
00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:11.478
so that it has the appropriate permissions.

7
00:00:11.578 --> 00:00:13.580
All right, let's get to it.

8
00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:17.384
There are three main types of identities for Databricks.

9
00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:21.388
There are your users. This is your user identities recognized by Databricks

10
00:00:21.388 --> 00:00:24.090
and they're represented by your email address.

11
00:00:24.391 --> 00:00:26.893
Service principals are to be used with

12
00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:29.396
jobs, automated tools, and systems such as

13
00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:32.098
scripts, apps, and CI/CD platforms.

14
00:00:32.098 --> 00:00:36.403
And then you have your groups. So groups simplify identity management,

15
00:00:36.403 --> 00:00:39.406
making it easier to assign access to workspaces,

16
00:00:39.406 --> 00:00:41.608
data, and other securable objects.

17
00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.677
So your groups can have sets of users,

18
00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:47.680
they can have service principals all assigned to them,

19
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:50.383
and that way you can just give access to a group

20
00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:53.053
if you're giving access to data, to models,

21
00:00:53.053 --> 00:00:56.856
anything you want to be able to grant permissions on.

22
00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:59.359
You can give it access to this entire group

23
00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:01.861
composed of users and service principals.

24
00:01:01.861 --> 00:01:04.030
For example, if you make a group

25
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.166
of developers and they need broad access.

26
00:01:06.199 --> 00:01:09.502
you can grant it easily instead of doing it one by one.

27
00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:14.107
There are also administrative roles that can manage Databricks identities.

28
00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:17.610
So this is your Account Admin, the highest level.

29
00:01:17.710 --> 00:01:21.014
They can add users, add service principals, and groups to accounts

30
00:01:21.014 --> 00:01:23.016
and assign admin roles.

31
00:01:23.216 --> 00:01:27.420
Workspace Admins add users and service principals to accounts,

32
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.023
and they can grant access to workspaces.

33
00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:34.360
So your Group Managers, those groups we just talked about,

34
00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.364
can manage the permissions within that group and assign group manager roles.

35
00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:40.567
And then you have your Service Principal Managers,

36
00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:43.369
so they manage the roles on those service principals.

37
00:01:44.070 --> 00:01:46.072
And those are identities for users,

38
00:01:46.072 --> 00:01:48.374
but what about identity management for agents?

39
00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:51.878
It's a challenge because an agent needs their own identity.

40
00:01:52.078 --> 00:01:54.080
An agent needs to run automated tasks

41
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:56.850
and it needs access to data in order to do so.

42
00:01:57.117 --> 00:02:01.588
and using a human admin credential will create security and audit risk.

43
00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:05.325
So they need consistent identity regardless of who triggers the agent.

44
00:02:05.725 --> 00:02:09.129
A solution to this is by creating a service principal

45
00:02:09.129 --> 00:02:11.197
so that when you run an agent,

46
00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:13.800
you run it with the credentials of a service principal.

47
00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.004
So you'd first create this dedicated service principal for each agent.

48
00:02:18.204 --> 00:02:23.109
You'd grant minimum required Unity Catalog permissions for that service principal,

49
00:02:23.209 --> 00:02:25.011
or if that service principal is in

50
00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.080
a group, make sure the permissions match up.

51
00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.783
And then you deploy the agent with specific identity context.

52
00:02:31.284 --> 00:02:35.388
The authentication options for agents during deployments are one,

53
00:02:35.388 --> 00:02:39.459
automatic authentication passthrough or system authentication.

54
00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:44.459
So the agent service principal gets automatic access to declared resources.

55
00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:48.468
So this means it's the same identity regardless of who calls it.

56
00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:51.271
It has managed credentials automatically.

57
00:02:51.271 --> 00:02:54.007
And this is most common if you're building

58
00:02:54.007 --> 00:02:57.076
an agent as a POC or as a demo.

59
00:02:57.076 --> 00:03:01.181
Two, On-Behalf-of-User Authentication. So agents use

60
00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:05.218
the end user's credentials, so has the caller's identity.

61
00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:08.721
It's good for user specific sensitive data access,

62
00:03:08.721 --> 00:03:11.724
and this requires specific API scopes.

63
00:03:11.724 --> 00:03:14.027
If you need to validate your identity

64
00:03:14.027 --> 00:03:16.229
and you can only be a real

65
00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.431
human user in order to do that,

66
00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:21.434
you would be able to validate that with the caller's identity.

67
00:03:21.434 --> 00:03:24.404
So that's the on behalf of user authentication.

68
00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:26.706
And the third is manual authentication.

69
00:03:26.706 --> 00:03:29.409
So this is where you have a pre-created service principal

70
00:03:29.409 --> 00:03:31.110
with specific permissions.

71
00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:35.348
and what some orgs refer to as CENTRALIZED identity management.

72
00:03:35.348 --> 00:03:37.517
This is very common for companies that

73
00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:39.652
put a lot of systems into production

74
00:03:39.852 --> 00:03:42.755
because there's no on-the-fly service principal creation.

75
00:03:43.056 --> 00:03:45.959
And for this lab, since it's what we see very commonly

76
00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:48.161
for models in production and now for what we're seeing

77
00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:51.764
agents going into production use is Manual Authentication.

78
00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:56.369
So we are going to pre-create the service principal with specific permissions,

79
00:03:56.669 --> 00:03:59.973
and this will create a CENTRALIZED identity management

80
00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:02.041
so that we can deploy the agent

81
00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:04.110
with the credentials of the service principal.

82
00:04:04.210 --> 00:04:06.112
And you'll see that in the following labs.

83
00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:09.115
With Manual Authentication, we are going to first

84
00:04:09.115 --> 00:04:11.618
in lab one create the service principal.

85
00:04:11.718 --> 00:04:14.120
We're also going to create a group of developers

86
00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.623
and add the service principal to that group.

87
00:04:16.723 --> 00:04:20.093
We are going to make sure that group has very specific permissions

88
00:04:20.193 --> 00:04:23.596
so that when we develop the agent in lab two and

89
00:04:23.696 --> 00:04:25.632
deploy the agent in lab three,

90
00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:27.900
we are going to end up deploying

91
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.603
the agent with the credentials of the service principal.

92
00:04:31.037 --> 00:04:33.039
Before we dive into this lab,

93
00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:34.941
if you'd like to follow along, you

94
00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:36.843
can create a Databricks free edition account

95
00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.245
and follow along with me on the lab steps.

96
00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:41.848
When you create a Databricks free edition account,

97
00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:43.850
you will have both admin privileges,

98
00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.853
so account admin as well as workspace admin.

99
00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:51.024
But just so you know, the account admin console is locked.

100
00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:54.160
So you won't have account admin privileges,

101
00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:56.195
and this is just because it is

102
00:04:56.195 --> 00:04:58.197
a free edition. Now let's get started.
